{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>And you shall be holy men to Me.</b> If you are holy and separated from the abominations of carcasses and torn flesh, then you are Mine; and if not, you are not Mine." "<b>And flesh in the field torn.</b> Also in the house, the same — but Scripture speaks of what is customary: the place where cattle are ordinarily torn. And so, ״כִּי בַשָּׂדֶה מְצָאָהּ״ (Deuteronomy 22), and so ״אֲשֶׁר לֹא יִהְיֶה טָהוֹר מִקְּרֵה לָיְלָה״ (Deuteronomy 23) — the same applies to an occurrence by day, but Scripture speaks of what is customary. And flesh torn from a living animal — flesh that was torn by means of the tearing of a wolf or lion from a clean wild animal or from a clean domestic animal while it is still alive." "<b>To the dog you shall cast it.</b> The gentile too is like the dog; or is it nothing but a dog, as its literal meaning? Scripture says, ״בִּנְבֵלָה אוֹ מָכֹר לְנָכְרִי״ (Deuteronomy 14) — all the more so torn flesh, which is permitted for any benefit. If so, what does Scripture tell you by ״לַכֶּלֶב״? To teach you that the dog is esteemed more than it, and Scripture teaches you that the Holy One, blessed be He, does not withhold the reward of any creature, as it says, ״וּלְכָל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל לֹא יֶחֱרַץ כֶּלֶב לְשֹׁנוֹ״ (Exodus 11). The Holy One, blessed be He, said: give him his reward (Mekhilta)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:54:04.868846978Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}